\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}
\usepackage[margin=25mm]{geometry}
\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm,array}
\setlength{\emergencystretch}{2em}
\newtheorem{lemma}{Lemma}\newtheorem{theorem}[lemma]{Theorem}
\title{Twenty-three hexagons through every edge}
\author{Human Compression project}\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle
\begin{theorem}
There is no finite simple quartic graph of girth six in which every
edge belongs to precisely twenty-three hexagons. Consequently
$n(4,6,23)=\infty$.
\end{theorem}
We reduce the assertion to explicit finite local configurations. The
certificate consists of short cycles, vertex permutations and unequal
integer pair counts. Its verifier uses only the Python standard library.

Fix an edge $uv$ in a hypothetical graph. The two trees of depth two
rooted at $u,v$, excluding $uv$ at their first steps, have thirteen
vertices each and are disjoint. Every additional edge within their
union joins a left leaf to a right leaf: any other edge or identification
creates a cycle of length at most five. These cross edges correspond
bijectively to hexagons through $uv$, so there are twenty-three.

On each side partition the nine leaves into three branches of size
three. Cross edges between a fixed pair of branches form a matching;
two cross neighbours in one branch would create a quadrangle.
Let $m_{ij}$ be its size and put $d_{ij}=3-m_{ij}$. Then
\[
 0\le d_{ij}\le3,\qquad \sum_{i,j=0}^2d_{ij}=4.       \tag{1}
\]
There are four unfinished leaf incidences on each side. Every such
incidence leads outside the twenty-six vertices, since all internal
edges have already been included.

\begin{lemma}\label{profile}
On either side the positive unfinished leaf degrees have one of the
two profiles $(2,1,1)$ and $(3,1)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Write $r_1,\ldots,r_9$ for the unfinished degrees of the right leaves.
Each is at most three and their sum is four. The number of hexagons
through $u$ avoiding $uv$ is twenty-three: all four incident edges
belong to twenty-three hexagons, so there are forty-six through $u$,
of which twenty-three use $uv$.

A right leaf with $3-r_i$ cross neighbours supplies
$\binom{3-r_i}{2}$ such hexagons, since these neighbours lie in
different left branches. The remaining hexagons have their opposite
vertex outside the tree union. Their required number is
\[
 23-\sum_i\binom{3-r_i}{2}
          =6-\frac12\sum_i r_i^2.                      \tag{2}
\]
The same formula holds with left and right interchanged.

For a side with unfinished-degree profile $P$, let $b(P)$ be an
upper bound on the pairs of its leaves sharing an outside neighbour.
There are only four incidences. No outside vertex can meet two leaves
in the same branch, as that would create a quadrangle. Thus an outside
vertex meets at most three leaves on this side, giving a universal
upper bound three. For profiles $(2,2)$ and $(3,1)$ there are just
two deficient leaves. They have at most one common outside neighbour,
again because two would create a quadrangle. We obtain
\[
\begin{array}{c|rrrr}
P&(1,1,1,1)&(2,1,1)&(2,2)&(3,1)\\\hline
6-\tfrac12\sum r_i^2&4&3&2&1\\
b(P)&3&3&1&1.
\end{array}                                             \tag{3}
\]
If $P_L,P_R$ are the two profiles, formula (2) requires the middle
entry for $P_R$ to be at most $b(P_L)$, and conversely.
The profile $(1,1,1,1)$ is therefore impossible. If one profile is
$(2,2)$, its bound one forces the other to be $(3,1)$; the latter
has bound one and cannot supply the required two on the first side.
This excludes $(2,2)$ as well. The two stated profiles remain.
We need only this necessary conclusion; the subsequent inventory may
include additional impossible combinations of the two profiles.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{pairs}
Let $a_i$ be the number of cross edges incident with left branch $i$.
For distinct $i,j,k\in\{0,1,2\}$ the number of hexagons through
the two left root edges of branches $i,j$ must equal $a_k$.
The analogous assertion holds on the right.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $b_{ij}$ count the former hexagons. The three root-edge equations
are $a_i+b_{ij}+b_{ik}=23$, and $a_0+a_1+a_2=23$.
Solving gives $b_{ij}=a_k$. Once every leaf has all its neighbours,
these counts cannot be changed by any farther completion. Explicitly,
if $L_i$ is its three-element leaf set, then
\[
             b_{ij}=\sum_w |N(w)\cap L_i|\,|N(w)\cap L_j|.       \tag{4}
\]
Every summand specifies the middle vertex of a length-four path
between the two branch vertices. Girth six ensures that these are
exactly the hexagons in question, without repeated vertices. All
neighbours of the leaves have already been included in (4).
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Complete local inventory.}
There are $486$ matrices in (1). Permuting the three left branches,
the three right branches, and interchanging the two sides reduces
them to fifteen representatives. The verifier enumerates all matrices
and all these transformations directly, retaining the least matrix
in each orbit. No classification premise is used here.

For each representative consider the bipartite graph on the six
branches whose edges are the complete matchings, namely $d_{ij}=0$.
Choose its spanning forest in lexicographic order. Independent
permutations of the three leaves within each branch make every forest
matching the identity: start in each component and choose the labels
of a new branch as it is reached. A forest has no consistency cycle,
so this procedure never conflicts with an earlier choice. All these
permutations are automorphisms of the original rooted trees.

Every other matching is freely enumerated. A matching of size
$3,2,1,0$ between two three-element sets has respectively
$6,18,9,1$ choices. Taking their products over the fifteen matrices
gives $1\,774\,224$ normalized possibilities. The certificate visits
this complete product by prefixes. A prefix is discarded only with
an explicit quadrangle; otherwise every next matching is visited.
At a complete choice Lemma~\ref{profile} may discard an impossible
profile. Each remaining graph is carried to one of twenty printed
adjacency lists by a supplied permutation of its twenty-six vertices.
The verifier checks every adjacency entry and that the unordered
root edge is preserved. The lists are in \texttt{representatives.json};
their completeness follows from these checked maps, independently of
the software used to discover them.

For each of the twenty representatives list its eight unfinished
incidences, repeating a leaf when necessary. Partition this list
according to the outside vertex at the other end of each incidence.
No part has more than four members and no part contains the same leaf
twice. These are the only preliminary restrictions. The verifier
generates every such partition by placing the next incidence in each
eligible existing part or in one new part. Ordering new parts by their
first appearance loses no partition. Some partitions of distinguishable
incidences give the same graph; this harmless redundancy is retained.

Adjoin one vertex for every part and all its specified edges to the
leaves. Every original tree vertex now has degree four. Edges between
these outside vertices, or to farther vertices, need not be enumerated:
they cannot remove a short cycle or change (4). For every partition
the second certificate supplies either a cycle of length three through
five or a side and a pair of branches for which (4) differs from the
required $a_k$. The verifier recomputes these integer counts. Its
complete inventory is
\[
\begin{array}{lr}
\text{Visited matching prefixes}&52424\\
\text{Prefixes rejected by a quadrangle}&43005\\
\text{Complete tree unions of girth at least six}&6420\\
\text{Rejected degree profiles}&5670\\
\text{Literal maps to the twenty representatives}&750\\
\text{Outside incidence partitions}&48816\\
\text{Partitions rejected by a short cycle}&44371\\
\text{Partitions rejected by equation (4)}&4445.
\end{array}
\]
The product coverage, exact prefix order and complete consumption of
both certificate files are checked. Thus no case is accepted merely
because a search program failed to return one. The two exhaustive
local steps contradict every hypothetical graph, proving the theorem.

\paragraph{Attribution and scope.}
Goedgebeur and Jooken, \emph{Exhaustive generation of edge-girth-regular
graphs}, arXiv:2401.08271v2, Table 2 on physical page 18, record
$n(4,6,23)\ge42$ without a finite upper example. The theorem strengthens
that bound to nonexistence at every order. The trees about an edge
are the classical Moore-bound construction. The present proof combines
the coupled degree-profile restriction, exact root-pair counts and
the finite matching and incidence inventories. The literal verification
is a proof dependency. No priority, whole-source completion, Lean or
numerical-compression claim is made here.
